    "debrief":  {
                    "realTeam":  "In April 2005, Opportunity drove into a soft sand ripple later named Purgatory Dune and its wheels dug in. The team practiced on Earth with a copy of the rover, then drove it out a few centimeters at a time. It took about five weeks. In 2009 Spirit got stuck in soft soil at a place called Troy. The team tried for months, but Spirit never got out, so it became a science station that stayed in one place.",
                    "tryNext":  "Collect samples early, while your oxygen is high, and never spin the wheels in soft sand.",
                    "realScience":  {
                                        "title":  "Why soft sand traps rovers",
                                        "text":  "Loose, fluffy sand can\u0027t push back on the wheels. Instead of rolling forward, they spin and dig themselves deeper. That is why rover drivers back out slowly, a tiny bit at a time.",
                                        "glossary":  "softsand"
                                    },
                    "sources":  [
                                    "mer_history",
                                    "nasa_images"
                                ]
                },
